# =============================================================================
# subscriber-usage — crontab example
# =============================================================================
#
# HOW TO INSTALL (Linux / macOS)
#   1. Open your crontab for editing:
#        crontab -e
#   2. Paste the block of your choice (Option A or Option B) below.
#   3. Adjust /path/to/subscriber-usage and /path/to/node to match your system.
#      Find the node path with:  which node
#   4. Create the logs directory first:
#        mkdir -p /path/to/subscriber-usage/logs
#
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTION A — using CRON_TZ (simpler, requires a cron daemon that supports it,
#             e.g. vixie-cron on most modern Linux distros and macOS)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

CRON_TZ=Asia/Jakarta

# Snapshot: 08:00, 12:00, 15:00 WIB
0  8 * * * /path/to/node /path/to/subscriber-usage/scripts/snapshot.js >> /path/to/subscriber-usage/logs/snapshot.log 2>&1
0 12 * * * /path/to/node /path/to/subscriber-usage/scripts/snapshot.js >> /path/to/subscriber-usage/logs/snapshot.log 2>&1
0 15 * * * /path/to/node /path/to/subscriber-usage/scripts/snapshot.js >> /path/to/subscriber-usage/logs/snapshot.log 2>&1

# Cleanup: 01:00 WIB daily (removes snapshots older than 30 days)
0  1 * * * /path/to/node /path/to/subscriber-usage/scripts/cleanup.js  >> /path/to/subscriber-usage/logs/snapshot.log 2>&1

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTION B — UTC-converted times (use this if your cron daemon ignores CRON_TZ)
#
#   WIB is UTC+7, so subtract 7 hours:
#     01:00 WIB → 18:00 UTC (previous day)   ← cleanup
#     08:00 WIB → 01:00 UTC
#     12:00 WIB → 05:00 UTC
#     15:00 WIB → 08:00 UTC
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Snapshot: 01:00, 05:00, 08:00 UTC  (= 08:00, 12:00, 15:00 WIB)
0  1 * * * /path/to/node /path/to/subscriber-usage/scripts/snapshot.js >> /path/to/subscriber-usage/logs/snapshot.log 2>&1
0  5 * * * /path/to/node /path/to/subscriber-usage/scripts/snapshot.js >> /path/to/subscriber-usage/logs/snapshot.log 2>&1
0  8 * * * /path/to/node /path/to/subscriber-usage/scripts/snapshot.js >> /path/to/subscriber-usage/logs/snapshot.log 2>&1

# Cleanup: 18:00 UTC daily (= 01:00 WIB next day)
0 18 * * * /path/to/node /path/to/subscriber-usage/scripts/cleanup.js  >> /path/to/subscriber-usage/logs/snapshot.log 2>&1

# =============================================================================
# WINDOWS — Task Scheduler
# =============================================================================
#
# Windows does not have cron, but Task Scheduler provides equivalent
# functionality. Run the commands below in an elevated PowerShell prompt.
#
# Prerequisites:
#   - Replace C:\path\to\subscriber-usage with your actual project path.
#   - Replace C:\path\to\node.exe (find it with: where.exe node).
#   - The times below are in WIB; change the -At value to match your needs.
#     If your Windows clock is not set to WIB, adjust accordingly.
#
# --- Snapshot task (08:00 WIB) ---
# schtasks /Create /TN "SubscriberUsage\Snapshot0800" /TR "\"C:\path\to\node.exe\" \"C:\path\to\subscriber-usage\scripts\snapshot.js\"" /SC DAILY /ST 08:00 /F
#
# --- Snapshot task (12:00 WIB) ---
# schtasks /Create /TN "SubscriberUsage\Snapshot1200" /TR "\"C:\path\to\node.exe\" \"C:\path\to\subscriber-usage\scripts\snapshot.js\"" /SC DAILY /ST 12:00 /F
#
# --- Snapshot task (15:00 WIB) ---
# schtasks /Create /TN "SubscriberUsage\Snapshot1500" /TR "\"C:\path\to\node.exe\" \"C:\path\to\subscriber-usage\scripts\snapshot.js\"" /SC DAILY /ST 15:00 /F
#
# --- Cleanup task (01:00 WIB) ---
# schtasks /Create /TN "SubscriberUsage\Cleanup" /TR "\"C:\path\to\node.exe\" \"C:\path\to\subscriber-usage\scripts\cleanup.js\"" /SC DAILY /ST 01:00 /F
#
# To view registered tasks:
#   schtasks /Query /TN "SubscriberUsage" /FO LIST
#
# To delete a task:
#   schtasks /Delete /TN "SubscriberUsage\Snapshot0800" /F
#
# Note: Task Scheduler does not redirect output automatically.
# Wrap the command in a .bat file that appends to a log file, e.g.:
#   @echo off
#   "C:\path\to\node.exe" "C:\path\to\subscriber-usage\scripts\snapshot.js" >> "C:\path\to\subscriber-usage\logs\snapshot.log" 2>&1
# Then point the task at that .bat file instead.
